.TH SYS_NOTE 2
.SH NAME
sys_note_word, sys_note \- tell a process that something may be ready (SYS_NOTE_WORD, SYS_NOTE)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_note_word(volatile void *w);
long sys_note(int pid);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_note_word
registers the calling process's note word: a 32-bit word in its memory
(4-byte aligned).
.PP
.B sys_note
adds one to process
.IR pid 's
note word and wakes every thread sleeping on it with
.BR sys_futex (2):
"something you wait for may be ready". The process then looks again. A
server tells a client so, without the client having to wait in a call;
it is the base of
.B poll
in the POSIX layer. The kernel does it too when one of the process's
children ends.
.PP
Only root, or a process of the same user, may note a process.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.RB ( sys_note_word )
The word is not aligned, or not a user address.
.TP
.B ESRCH
.RB ( sys_note )
No such process, or it has no note word.
.TP
.B EPERM
.RB ( sys_note )
It belongs to another user, and the caller is not root.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.RB ( sys_note )
Its note word is not writable memory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_futex (2),
.BR sys_wait (2),
.BR unixd (4)
